/* Power Up — questions en anglais (dev). Clé = question française + « ¦ » + première réponse. */
PU_I18N.questions({
  "En Python, qu'est-ce qui délimite un bloc de code (dans un if, une boucle…) ? ¦ Les accolades { } autour du bloc": {"q":"In Python, what delimits a block of code (in an if, a loop…)?","e":"Python enforces indentation: a wrong offset causes an error or changes the program's logic.","a":["The curly braces { } around the block","Indentation (the offset of the lines)","The keywords begin … end"]},
  "Git sert à… ¦ Suivre l'historique des versions d'un code et collaborer": {"q":"Git is used to…","e":"Git records every change (commit). GitHub or GitLab host repositories online, just like your TSSR labs!","a":["Track the version history of code and collaborate","Automatically back up your computer to the cloud","Find out who ate the last croissant in the open space"]},
  "Envoyer une modification sur GitHub, dans l'ordre : ¦ Modifier le fichier": {"q":"Push a change to GitHub, in the right order:","e":"add stages the changes, commit saves them locally with a message, push sends them to the remote repository.","items":["Edit the file","git add fichier","git commit -m \"message\"","git push"]},
  "En Python, que vaut ma_liste[0] si ma_liste = [\"Teams\", \"Outlook\", \"Excel\"] ? ¦ \"Teams\"": {"q":"In Python, what is ma_liste[0] if ma_liste = [\"Teams\", \"Outlook\", \"Excel\"]?","e":"Indexes start at 0: [0] is the first element, [2] the last. len(ma_liste) is 3.","a":["\"Teams\"","\"Outlook\"","An error"]},
  "Que fait cette requête SQL : SELECT nom FROM utilisateurs WHERE service = 'IT'; ? ¦ Compte les utilisateurs du service IT": {"q":"What does this SQL query do: SELECT nom FROM utilisateurs WHERE service = 'IT'; ?","e":"SELECT chooses the columns (here only nom; * would take them all), FROM the table, WHERE filters the rows. To count, you would write SELECT COUNT(*). DELETE, INSERT and UPDATE modify data.","a":["Counts the users in the IT department","Displays the names of the users in the IT department","Displays all columns of the users in the IT department"]},
  "Un fichier .gitignore empêche certains fichiers (mots de passe, fichiers temporaires) d'être envoyés dans le dépôt. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"A .gitignore file prevents certain files (passwords, temporary files) from being sent to the repository.","e":"True. But be careful: a secret that has already been committed stays in the history. You then have to revoke it and generate a new one."},
  "Comment se protéger des injections SQL ? ¦ En utilisant des requêtes paramétrées": {"q":"How do you protect against SQL injection?","e":"Parameterized queries separate the SQL code from the data entered by the user: a “' OR 1=1 --” is then treated as text, not as code. Hiding errors or encrypting the connection does nothing against injection.","a":["By using parameterized queries","By hiding SQL error messages","By encrypting the connection to the database (TLS)"]},
  "Trouve la faille dans ce code Python : ¦ nom = input(\"Nom : \")": {"q":"Find the flaw in this Python code:","e":"The user's input is pasted directly into the query: SQL injection is possible. The best practice: curseur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM clients WHERE nom = ?\", (nom,)).","lines":[[{"t":"nom = input(\"Nom : \")","c":false}],[{"t":"requete = ","c":false},{"t":"\"SELECT * FROM clients WHERE nom = '\" + nom + \"'\"","c":true}],[{"t":"curseur.execute(requete)","c":false}]]},
  "En programmation, une variable, c'est… ¦ Une instruction qui répète une action": {"q":"In programming, a variable is…","e":"xp = 120 creates a variable xp equal to 120. Its value can change as the program runs, hence its name.","a":["An instruction that repeats an action","A named box that stores a value","A colleague who changes their mind every five minutes"]},
  "Qu'affiche print(2 + 3 * 4) en Python ? ¦ 20": {"q":"What does print(2 + 3 * 4) display in Python?","e":"Just like in math, multiplication comes before addition: 3 × 4 = 12, then 2 + 12 = 14. With parentheses, (2 + 3) * 4 would give 20.","a":["20","234","14"]},
  "Un commentaire est ignoré à l'exécution et sert à expliquer le code aux humains. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"A comment is ignored at execution and is used to explain the code to humans.","e":"True. In Python and Bash it starts with #, in PowerShell too, in JavaScript with //. Your future self will thank you."},
  "Qu'affiche ce code Python : for i in range(3): print(i) ¦ 0, 1 et 2": {"q":"What does this Python code display: for i in range(3): print(i)","e":"range(3) produces 0, 1, 2: it starts at 0 and stops before 3. It's the same logic as list indexes.","a":["0, 1 and 2","1, 2 and 3","0, 1, 2 and 3"]},
  "Une branche Git sert à… ¦ Revenir à une ancienne version en effaçant les commits suivants": {"q":"A Git branch is used to…","e":"git switch -c nouvelle-page creates a branch. Once the work is validated, it is merged into the main branch (main).","a":["Go back to an older version by deleting the following commits","Work on a feature separately, without breaking the main version","Copy a remote repository to your computer with its full history"]},
  "Une fonction, c'est… ¦ Une boîte nommée qui stocke une valeur modifiable au fil du programme": {"q":"A function is…","e":"def bonjour(nom): return \"Bonjour \" + nom. You write it once and use it everywhere: less copy-paste, fewer bugs.","a":["A named box that stores a value that can change as the program runs","An instruction that repeats a block while a condition is true","A reusable block of code that you call by name, with parameters"]},
  "La commande git clone sert à… ¦ Copier un dépôt distant sur son poste, avec tout son historique": {"q":"The git clone command is used to…","e":"git clone https://github.com/… gets the project the first time. After that, git pull fetches new changes and git push sends yours; git branch creates a branch.","a":["Copy a remote repository to your computer, with its full history","Create a new branch from the current branch","Fetch the latest commits from the remote repository into your local copy"]},
  "Une pull request (ou merge request)… ¦ Récupère les modifications du dépôt distant dans ta branche locale": {"q":"A pull request (or merge request)…","e":"Colleagues comment, automated tests run, then the branch is merged. Despite its name, it is not a git pull, nor a fork (the copy of the repository under your account). It's also an excellent way to learn.","a":["Fetches the changes from the remote repository into your local branch","Proposes merging a branch, allowing the code to be reviewed first","Creates a copy of the repository under your own GitHub account"]},
  "En SQL, quelle clause trie les résultats ? ¦ GROUP BY": {"q":"In SQL, which clause sorts the results?","e":"SELECT nom FROM utilisateurs ORDER BY nom ASC sorts alphabetically (DESC for reverse order). GROUP BY groups, WHERE filters.","a":["GROUP BY","WHERE","ORDER BY"]},
  "Un conflit de fusion (merge conflict) dans Git arrive quand… ¦ Deux branches ont modifié les mêmes lignes d'un fichier et Git ne sait pas quelle version garder": {"q":"A merge conflict in Git happens when…","e":"Git marks the area with <<<<<<< and >>>>>>>. You choose the right version (or combine both), then make a commit. Different files merge on their own, and a rejected push requires a pull first, without necessarily involving a conflict.","a":["Two branches have modified the same lines of a file and Git doesn't know which version to keep","Two branches have modified different files in the same folder since they diverged","The remote repository contains commits that you haven't fetched yet when you push"]},
  "Chercher un élément dans une liste non triée de n éléments demande, au pire… ¦ log₂(n) comparaisons, soit O(log n)": {"q":"Searching for an item in an unsorted list of n items requires, in the worst case…","e":"You may have to go through everything. In a sorted list, binary search needs only about log₂(n) comparisons: around twenty for a million items.","a":["log₂(n) comparisons, i.e. O(log n)","n comparisons, i.e. O(n) complexity","n² comparisons, i.e. O(n²)"]},
  "Quelle commande Git envoie tes commits vers le dépôt distant ? ¦ git push": {"q":"Which Git command sends your commits to the remote repository?","e":"git push sends, git pull fetches. Before pushing, git status and git log let you check what is going out.","placeholder":"Command","answers":["git push"]},
  "En SQL, quel mot-clé filtre les lignes selon une condition ? ¦ WHERE": {"q":"In SQL, which keyword filters rows based on a condition?","e":"SELECT nom FROM utilisateurs WHERE service = 'IT'. To filter after a GROUP BY, you use HAVING.","placeholder":"Keyword","answers":["WHERE"]},
  "En Python, quel est le type de la valeur \"42\" (avec les guillemets) ? ¦ str (chaîne de caractères)": {"q":"In Python, what is the type of the value \"42\" (with the quotes)?","e":"The quotes make it text. int(\"42\") converts it to an integer: this is often necessary with input(), which always returns a string.","a":["str (string)","int (integer)","float (decimal number)"]},
  "Quelle commande Git affiche les fichiers modifiés, indexés ou non suivis de ton dépôt ? ¦ git status": {"q":"Which Git command displays the modified, staged or untracked files in your repository?","e":"git status is the reflex before every commit: it shows what will be saved (staged with git add) and what is not yet.","placeholder":"git …","answers":["git status","status"]},
  "À quoi sert un index sur une colonne d'une table SQL ? ¦ À chiffrer les données de la colonne sur le disque": {"q":"What is an index on a column of an SQL table used for?","e":"Like a book's index, it avoids scanning the whole table for a WHERE or a join. In exchange, every INSERT or UPDATE must also update the index.","a":["Encrypting the column's data on disk","Speeding up searches on that column","Automatically numbering each new row"]},
  "Lesquels sont des types natifs de Python, utilisables sans aucun import ? ¦ list": {"q":"Which of these are native Python types, usable without any import?","e":"list [1, 2], dict {\"cle\": \"valeur\"} and tuple (1, 2) are built into the language. array requires import array (or the NumPy library).","a":["list","dict","array","tuple"]},
  "Quelle jointure renvoie TOUS les clients, y compris ceux sans commande (colonnes de commande à NULL) ?\nSELECT c.nom, o.id FROM clients c ??? commandes o ON o.client_id = c.id; ¦ INNER JOIN": {"q":"Which join returns ALL customers, including those without an order (order columns set to NULL)?\nSELECT c.nom, o.id FROM clients c ??? commandes o ON o.client_id = c.id;","e":"LEFT JOIN keeps all the rows of the left table (clients). INNER JOIN would only keep customers with at least one order, and CROSS JOIN would produce every possible combination.","a":["INNER JOIN","CROSS JOIN","LEFT JOIN"]},
  "Quelle est la différence entre git merge et git rebase ? ¦ merge crée un commit de fusion ; rebase rejoue tes commits et réécrit l'historique": {"q":"What is the difference between git merge and git rebase?","e":"Rebase gives a linear history, but the replayed commits get new hashes. Golden rule: don't rebase a shared branch that has already been pushed, or you'll break your colleagues' work.","a":["merge creates a merge commit; rebase replays your commits and rewrites the history","rebase deletes the source branch and all its commits once the merge is done","merge rewrites the history; rebase keeps the original commits intact"]},
  "Dans quel ordre logique une base SQL évalue-t-elle les clauses d'une requête avec agrégation ? ¦ FROM (et les JOIN)": {"q":"In what logical order does an SQL database evaluate the clauses of a query with aggregation?","e":"WHERE filters rows before grouping, HAVING filters groups after aggregation: that's why COUNT(*) > 10 goes in HAVING and not in WHERE. An alias defined in SELECT is not yet known in WHERE.","items":["FROM (and the JOINs)","WHERE","GROUP BY","HAVING","SELECT"]},
  "Après un git reset --hard malheureux, quelle commande affiche l'historique des positions de HEAD pour retrouver les commits « perdus » ? ¦ git reflog": {"q":"After an unfortunate git reset --hard, which command displays the history of HEAD positions so you can find the “lost” commits?","e":"git reflog locally lists all HEAD movements (commits, resets, rebases, checkouts). You then recover the commit with git reset --hard HEAD@{1} or by creating a branch on its hash.","placeholder":"git …","answers":["git reflog","reflog","git reflog show"]},
  "En Python, quel mot-clé sert à définir une fonction ? ¦ function": {"q":"In Python, which keyword is used to define a function?","e":"You write def nom_fonction(paramètres): followed by the indented body. function is the JavaScript keyword, fn the Rust one: Python doesn't recognize them.","a":["function","func","def","fn"]},
  "Que renvoie la requête SQL SELECT * FROM clients; ? ¦ Le nombre de lignes de la table clients": {"q":"What does the SQL query SELECT * FROM clients; return?","e":"The asterisk means “all columns”, and without a WHERE no row is filtered out. To count rows, you would use SELECT COUNT(*) FROM clients;.","a":["The number of rows in the clients table","The structure of the table (column names and types)","Only the first row of the table","All columns of all rows of the clients table"]},
  "En Python, l'instruction x = 5 vérifie si x est égal à 5. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"In Python, the statement x = 5 checks whether x is equal to 5.","e":"A single = is an assignment: it stores the value 5 in x. To compare, you use == (for example if x == 5:)."},
  "Quelle commande Git transforme le dossier courant en nouveau dépôt Git ? ¦ git init": {"q":"Which Git command turns the current folder into a new Git repository?","e":"git init creates the hidden .git subfolder that will hold the whole history. To get a repository that already exists elsewhere, you use git clone instead.","placeholder":"git …","answers":["git init","init"]},
  "Dans Git, un commit, c'est… ¦ L'envoi de tes modifications vers le dépôt distant sur GitHub": {"q":"In Git, a commit is…","e":"A commit records the state of the staged files in the local history, with a unique identifier. Sending to GitHub is a separate step: git push.","a":["Sending your changes to the remote repository on GitHub","A snapshot of your changes, with a message, author and date","An automatic backup copy made every hour","Merging your branch into the project's main branch"]},
  "Lesquelles de ces commandes existent réellement dans Git ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ git commit": {"q":"Which of these commands actually exist in Git? (several answers)","e":"git commit saves changes, git branch manages branches and git merge merges them. git deploy doesn't exist: deployment is handled by other tools (CI/CD pipelines).","a":["git commit","git deploy","git branch","git merge"]},
  "Que renvoie len(\"Power Up\") en Python ? ¦ 8": {"q":"What does len(\"Power Up\") return in Python?","e":"len() counts the characters of a string, including the space: P-o-w-e-r, the space, U-p, so 8.","a":["8","7","2","\"Power Up\""]},
  "Que renvoie SELECT service, COUNT(*) FROM employes GROUP BY service; ? ¦ Le nombre total d'employés, sur une seule ligne": {"q":"What does SELECT service, COUNT(*) FROM employes GROUP BY service; return?","e":"GROUP BY groups the rows that have the same service value, and COUNT(*) counts the rows in each group. You therefore get one row per department.","a":["The total number of employees, on a single row","The list of all employees sorted by department","One row per department, with its number of employees","The number of different departments"]},
  "git revert <commit> crée un nouveau commit qui annule les modifications d'un commit précédent, sans réécrire l'historique. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"git revert <commit> creates a new commit that undoes the changes of a previous commit, without rewriting the history.","e":"revert adds an “inverse” commit: the history stays intact, which makes it safe on a shared branch. By contrast, git reset moves the branch and can make commits disappear."},
  "Remets dans l'ordre : créer une branche, y enregistrer un correctif puis le fusionner dans main. ¦ git switch -c correctif": {"q":"Put in order: create a branch, save a fix on it, then merge it into main.","e":"You create and switch to the branch (switch -c), stage then commit on that branch. Then you go back to main and merge the correctif branch into it.","items":["git switch -c correctif","git add .","git commit -m \"Corrige le bug\"","git switch main","git merge correctif"]},
  "Avec conf = {\"port\": 443}, que renvoie conf.get(\"host\") ? ¦ None": {"q":"With conf = {\"port\": 443}, what does conf.get(\"host\") return?","e":"dict.get() returns None (or the default value passed as the second argument) when the key is missing. It's conf[\"host\"] that would raise a KeyError.","a":["None","A KeyError","An empty string \"\"","False"]},
  "Après un GROUP BY service, comment ne garder que les services de plus de 10 employés ? ¦ WHERE COUNT(*) > 10": {"q":"After a GROUP BY service, how do you keep only the departments with more than 10 employees?","e":"WHERE filters rows before grouping and cannot contain an aggregate; HAVING filters groups after aggregates such as COUNT(*) have been computed.","a":["WHERE COUNT(*) > 10","LIMIT 10","HAVING COUNT(*) > 10","ORDER BY COUNT(*) > 10"]},
  "Quelle commande Git met de côté tes modifications non commitées pour te laisser changer de branche, puis les restaurer plus tard ? ¦ git stash": {"q":"Which Git command sets aside your uncommitted changes so you can switch branches, then restore them later?","e":"git stash puts the current changes on a stack and leaves the working directory clean. git stash pop then reapplies them.","placeholder":"git …","answers":["git stash","stash","git stash push"]},
  "Lesquels de ces types Python sont immuables (non modifiables après création) ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ tuple": {"q":"Which of these Python types are immutable (cannot be modified after creation)? (several answers)","e":"tuple and str cannot be modified: any “modification” creates a new object. list and dict are mutable, which is why a dict cannot be used as a dictionary key.","a":["tuple","list","str","dict"]},
  "Que vaut [x * 2 for x in range(4) if x % 2 == 0] en Python ? ¦ [0, 2, 4, 6]": {"q":"What is [x * 2 for x in range(4) if x % 2 == 0] in Python?","e":"range(4) gives 0, 1, 2, 3; the condition keeps only the even ones, 0 and 2, which are then multiplied by 2. Result: [0, 4].","a":["[0, 2, 4, 6]","[4, 8]","[2, 6]","[0, 4]"]},
  "Qu'affiche ce code Python ?\ndef ajoute(x, l=[]):\nl.append(x)\nreturn l\najoute(1)\nprint(ajoute(2)) ¦ [2]": {"q":"What does this Python code display?\ndef ajoute(x, l=[]):\n    l.append(x)\n    return l\najoute(1)\nprint(ajoute(2))","e":"The default value [] is created only once, when the function is defined, and then shared between calls. The right reflex: l=None then if l is None: l = [] in the body.","a":["[2]","[1]","[1, 2]","[2, 1]"]},
  "En SQL, WHERE telephone = NULL renvoie les lignes dont le téléphone n'est pas renseigné. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"In SQL, WHERE telephone = NULL returns the rows whose phone number is not filled in.","e":"Any comparison with NULL gives UNKNOWN, never true: the query returns no rows. You have to write WHERE telephone IS NULL."},
  "La table contacts compte 10 lignes, dont 3 avec telephone à NULL. Que renvoie SELECT COUNT(telephone) FROM contacts; ? ¦ 10": {"q":"The contacts table has 10 rows, 3 of which have telephone set to NULL. What does SELECT COUNT(telephone) FROM contacts; return?","e":"COUNT(column) ignores NULL values, whereas COUNT(*) counts all rows. Here, 10 − 3 = 7.","a":["10","7","3","NULL"]},
  "Pourquoi évite-t-on de rebaser une branche déjà poussée et utilisée par des collègues ? ¦ Parce que rebase supprime la branche d'origine dès que ses commits ont été rejoués ailleurs": {"q":"Why do we avoid rebasing a branch that has already been pushed and is used by colleagues?","e":"Rebasing rewrites the history: the replayed commits have new SHAs. Colleagues who had the old version end up with a diverging history, hence the rule to only rebase commits that are still local.","a":["Because rebase deletes the original branch as soon as its commits have been replayed elsewhere","Because rebase only works on a branch that has never been pushed to the remote repository","Because rebase automatically merges the branch into main, skipping any review","Because rebase recreates the commits (new hashes): forced push and colleagues' copies out of sync"]},
  "Quelle option de git push force l'envoi, mais refuse si la branche distante a reçu des commits que tu n'as pas encore vus ? ¦ --force-with-lease": {"q":"Which git push option forces the push, but refuses if the remote branch has received commits that you haven't seen yet?","e":"--force-with-lease checks that the remote branch still points to the expected commit (your tracking reference) before overwriting. --force overwrites unconditionally and can erase a colleague's work.","placeholder":"--…","answers":["--force-with-lease","git push --force-with-lease","force-with-lease"]},
  "Lesquelles de ces valeurs sont évaluées comme fausses (falsy) dans un if en Python ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ []": {"q":"Which of these values evaluate as false (falsy) in a Python if? (several answers)","e":"Empty containers ([], \"\", {}), 0, None and False are falsy. \"0\" is a non-empty string, so it is truthy, even though it contains the zero character.","a":["[]","\"0\"","0","\"\""]},
  "Remets dans l'ordre la résolution d'un conflit lors d'un git merge : ¦ Lancer git merge feature : Git signale un conflit": {"q":"Put in order the resolution of a conflict during a git merge:","e":"git status lists the “both modified” files. Once the content is chosen and the markers removed, git add marks the conflict as resolved, then the merge commit completes the operation (git merge --abort lets you cancel as long as it isn't done).","items":["Run git merge feature: Git reports a conflict","Find the conflicting files with git status","Edit those files: keep the right content and remove the markers <<<<<<<, ======= and >>>>>>>","Stage the resolved files with git add","Finish with git commit (or git merge --continue)"]},
  "La table A contient 3 lignes avec client_id = 5 et la table B 2 lignes avec client_id = 5. Combien de lignes pour ce client renvoie A INNER JOIN B ON A.client_id = B.client_id ? ¦ 2": {"q":"Table A contains 3 rows with client_id = 5 and table B 2 rows with client_id = 5. How many rows for this customer does A INNER JOIN B ON A.client_id = B.client_id return?","e":"A join matches each row of A with each matching row of B: 3 × 2 = 6 rows. This is the classic cause of duplicates and inflated totals after a JOIN.","a":["2","3","6","5"]},
  "Pourquoi mettre à jour les bibliothèques (dépendances) d'un projet ? ¦ Pour corriger les failles de sécurité connues": {"q":"Why update a project's libraries (dependencies)?","e":"A large part of an application's code comes from external libraries. Tools like Dependabot on GitHub flag vulnerable versions.","a":["To fix known security flaws","So the project takes up less disk space","To have the same version as the intern, out of solidarity"]},
  "Il faut toujours vérifier les données saisies par l'utilisateur avant de s'en servir. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"You must always validate user-entered data before using it.","e":"True: type, length, format, allowed characters… “Never trust input” is the golden rule of secure development."},
  "Tu trouves un mot de passe dans un dépôt GitHub public de ton équipe. Tu… ¦ Le supprimes du fichier, et c'est réglé": {"q":"You find a password in your team's public GitHub repository. You…","e":"It stays in the Git history, and bots scan GitHub constantly: it may already have been copied. Making the repository private is therefore not enough; only changing the secret solves the problem.","a":["Delete it from the file, and that's that","Make the repository private so it is no longer visible","Immediately report it so it can be revoked and replaced"]},
  "Une faille XSS (cross-site scripting) permet… ¦ D'injecter du JavaScript malveillant qui s'exécute dans le navigateur des autres visiteurs": {"q":"A cross-site scripting (XSS) flaw allows…","e":"Example: a comment containing a script that steals session cookies. Defense: encode what you display and use a Content Security Policy (CSP).","a":["Injecting malicious JavaScript that runs in other visitors' browsers","Injecting SQL code into a form to read or modify the database","Making the server send requests to its own internal addresses"]},
  "Comment une application doit-elle stocker les mots de passe de ses utilisateurs ? ¦ Hachés en MD5, un algorithme standard et rapide à calculer": {"q":"How should an application store its users' passwords?","e":"The \"salt\" makes each hash unique, and the deliberate slowness hinders cracking attempts if the database is stolen. MD5 is far too fast, and encrypted data can be decrypted as soon as the key leaks.","a":["Hashed with MD5, a standard algorithm that is fast to compute","Hashed with a slow, salted algorithm such as bcrypt or Argon2","Encrypted with AES-256, with the key stored in the code"]},
  "Le « secure by design », c'est… ¦ Faire valider l'application par un pentest juste avant la mise en production": {"q":"\"Secure by design\" means…","e":"A flaw found at design time costs far less than one discovered in production… or by an attacker.","a":["Having the application validated by a pentest just before going into production","Adding an antivirus and a firewall once the application is finished","Building security in from the design stage, then testing it at every step of the project"]},
  "Pourquoi la revue de code aide-t-elle aussi la sécurité ? ¦ Un second regard repère des failles et des erreurs que l'auteur n'a pas vues": {"q":"Why does code review also help security?","e":"Forgotten secrets, unchecked inputs, overly broad permissions… A security checklist makes the review more effective.","a":["A second pair of eyes spots flaws and mistakes the author missed","It makes automated security testing such as SAST unnecessary","It checks that the code follows the team's naming conventions"]},
  "Le « SAST », c'est… ¦ Le test de l'application en fonctionnement, en l'attaquant de l'extérieur": {"q":"\"SAST\" is…","e":"DAST, on the other hand, tests the running application as an attacker would, and SCA inventories dependencies. The approaches complement each other.","a":["Testing the application while it is running, by attacking it from the outside","Analyzing the source code for flaws, without running it","An inventory of third-party libraries and their known vulnerabilities"]},
  "Un SBOM, c'est… ¦ La liste des vulnérabilités connues d'un logiciel et leur score CVSS": {"q":"An SBOM is…","e":"When a flaw affects a library, the SBOM lets you immediately tell which applications are concerned.","a":["The list of a software's known vulnerabilities and their CVSS score","The log of a software's versions and their release dates","The list of all the components and dependencies of a software"]},
  "La faille Log4Shell, révélée en décembre 2021, touchait… ¦ Log4j, une bibliothèque Java de journalisation très répandue": {"q":"The Log4Shell flaw, disclosed in December 2021, affected…","e":"Present in countless pieces of software, it allowed remote code execution through a simple logged text string. OpenSSL was Heartbleed in 2014. Many companies discovered they were using Log4j without knowing it.","a":["Log4j, a very widely used Java logging library","OpenSSL, a very widely used C encryption library","Apache Struts, a Java framework for web applications"]},
  "Trouve les 2 failles dans ce code Python : ¦ API_KEY = \"sk-live-8f3a91c2\"": {"q":"Find the 2 flaws in this Python code:","e":"The API key is hardcoded in the source: it will end up in Git. And the user input is inserted directly into the SQL query: injection is possible. Use an environment variable and a parameterized query.","lines":[[{"t":"API_KEY = \"sk-live-8f3a91c2\"","c":true}],[{"t":"def chercher_client(nom):","c":false}],[{"t":"    requete = ","c":false},{"t":"f\"SELECT * FROM clients WHERE nom = '{nom}'\"","c":true}],[{"t":"    return db.execute(requete)","c":false}]]},
  "Le moindre privilège appliqué au compte qu'une application utilise pour lire sa base de données : ¦ Le compte peut lire et écrire dans toutes les tables de la base": {"q":"Least privilege applied to the account an application uses to read its database:","e":"If the application is compromised, the attacker will be able neither to delete the tables nor to read the rest of the database.","a":["The account can read and write all tables in the database","The account only has read rights on the tables it needs","The account owns only the application's schema"]},
  "Une faille CSRF… ¦ Fait exécuter à un utilisateur connecté une action qu'il n'a pas voulue sur un site, depuis une autre page": {"q":"A CSRF flaw…","e":"Example: a booby-trapped page that secretly submits a form to change an email address. Defenses: anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies.","a":["Makes a logged-in user perform an action they did not intend on a site, from another page","Injects into a page a script that then runs in other visitors' browsers","Pushes the server to send requests to internal resources on the attacker's behalf"]},
  "Quel en-tête HTTP impose au navigateur de n'utiliser que HTTPS pour un site ? ¦ HSTS": {"q":"Which HTTP header forces the browser to use only HTTPS for a site?","e":"Strict-Transport-Security (HSTS): once received, the browser refuses to connect over plain HTTP, which blocks downgrade attacks.","placeholder":"Name or acronym","answers":["HSTS","Strict-Transport-Security"]},
  "Où ranger la clé d'API utilisée par un script d'automatisation ? ¦ En commentaire en haut du script, pour s'en souvenir": {"q":"Where should you keep the API key used by an automation script?","e":"A script gets copied, shared and versioned: a secret written in it ends up leaking. A secrets vault (Azure Key Vault…) centralizes secrets, logs access and lets you change the key without touching the code.","a":["In a comment at the top of the script, so you remember it","In a secrets vault or an environment variable","In the Git repository's README, under \"Configuration\""]},
  "Supprimer un mot de passe dans un nouveau commit suffit à le faire disparaître d'un dépôt Git. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"Deleting a password in a new commit is enough to make it disappear from a Git repository.","e":"It remains readable in the history of earlier commits, and may already have been copied. You must first revoke and replace the secret, then clean the history if needed."},
  "Que fait la commande npm audit dans un projet JavaScript ? ¦ Elle compare les dépendances du projet aux vulnérabilités connues publiées": {"q":"What does the npm audit command do in a JavaScript project?","e":"It flags vulnerable packages and their severity; npm audit fix suggests updates. Equivalents: pip-audit for Python, Dependabot on GitHub.","a":["It compares the project's dependencies against published known vulnerabilities","It compresses the dependencies to make the project lighter","It checks that the code follows style conventions"]},
  "Quelles informations ne doivent PAS apparaître dans un message d'erreur affiché à l'utilisateur d'une application web ? ¦ La pile d'appels complète (stack trace)": {"q":"Which information must NOT appear in an error message shown to the user of a web application?","e":"These details help an attacker map the application and its flaws. Show a generic message with an ID, and send the details to server-side logs.","a":["The full call stack (stack trace)","The SQL query that failed","An error ID to give to support","The exact version of the framework and the server"]},
  "Dans un projet Node.js géré avec npm, quel fichier fige les versions exactes de toutes les dépendances, y compris indirectes ? ¦ package-lock.json": {"q":"In a Node.js project managed with npm, which file pins the exact versions of all dependencies, including indirect ones?","e":"package.json specifies version ranges (^1.2.0); package-lock.json pins exact versions and their hashes. It is committed for reproducible installs (npm ci).","placeholder":"file name","answers":["package-lock.json","package-lock"]},
  "Une application télécharge une image depuis une URL saisie par l'utilisateur. Un attaquant saisit http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/. Quelle faille tente-t-il d'exploiter ? ¦ Une XSS stockée (script injecté dans la page)": {"q":"An application downloads an image from a URL entered by the user. An attacker enters http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/. Which flaw are they trying to exploit?","e":"In an SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery), the server makes the request on the attacker's behalf and can reach internal resources, such as the cloud metadata service, which sometimes exposes credentials. Defense: domain allowlist, blocking internal IPs, IMDSv2 on AWS.","a":["A stored XSS (script injected into the page)","A CSRF (request forged from the browser)","An SSRF (request forged on the server side)"]},
  "Quel en-tête HTTP restreint les sources depuis lesquelles le navigateur peut charger scripts, styles et images, limitant l'impact d'une XSS ? ¦ Strict-Transport-Security": {"q":"Which HTTP header restricts the sources from which the browser can load scripts, styles and images, limiting the impact of an XSS?","e":"A CSP such as script-src 'self' prevents injected scripts, or scripts from other domains, from running. HSTS enforces HTTPS, and Access-Control-Allow-Origin is part of CORS.","a":["Strict-Transport-Security","Content-Security-Policy","Access-Control-Allow-Origin"]},
  "Une API accepte un JWT dont l'en-tête indique \"alg\": \"none\" et dont la signature est vide. Quel est le risque ? ¦ N'importe qui peut forger un jeton avec \"role\": \"admin\"": {"q":"An API accepts a JWT whose header says \"alg\": \"none\" and whose signature is empty. What is the risk?","e":"The signature is the only integrity guarantee of a JWT, whose content is simply Base64URL-encoded. The server must enforce a list of expected algorithms (RS256, ES256…) and reject \"none\".","a":["Anyone can forge a token with \"role\": \"admin\"","None: the payload of a signed JWT is encrypted and unreadable","The token expires immediately and the user is logged out"]},
  "L'OWASP Top 10, c'est… ¦ une norme ISO obligatoire pour les sites web publics en Europe": {"q":"The OWASP Top 10 is…","e":"OWASP (Open Worldwide Application Security Project) publishes the Top 10, an awareness list of the major risks to web applications. It is neither a mandatory standard nor a tool.","a":["an ISO standard that is mandatory for public websites in Europe","a reference list of the major risks to web applications","the yearly ranking of the ten best antivirus products on the market","a free tool that automatically scans your website's code"]},
  "Une injection SQL consiste à… ¦ saturer une base de données avec des milliers de requêtes": {"q":"An SQL injection consists of…","e":"If the application pastes the user's input as-is into its query, text like ' OR '1'='1 changes the meaning of the query and can expose or modify data.","a":["flooding a database with thousands of requests","stealing the database file from the server","slipping SQL code into an input to hijack the query","guessing the database administrator's password"]},
  "Vérifier les champs d'un formulaire en JavaScript dans le navigateur suffit à valider les entrées de façon sûre. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"Checking form fields with JavaScript in the browser is enough to validate inputs safely.","e":"Browser-side checks improve usability but are easily bypassed (developer tools, request sent directly). Validation must always be redone on the server side."},
  "Pourquoi un formulaire de connexion doit-il être servi en HTTPS ? ¦ pour que la page de connexion se charge plus vite sur mobile": {"q":"Why must a login form be served over HTTPS?","e":"HTTPS encrypts traffic between the browser and the server. It does not handle database storage: hashing the password still has to be done on the server side.","a":["so the login page loads faster on mobile","to stop bots from filling in the form automatically","so the password is automatically stored hashed in the database","to encrypt exchanges and prevent the password from being intercepted"]},
  "Lesquels de ces éléments sont des secrets à ne jamais écrire en clair dans le code source ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ le mot de passe de la base de données": {"q":"Which of these items are secrets that must never be written in plain text in source code? (several answers)","e":"Passwords, API keys and private keys grant access: they belong in a secrets vault or environment variables. The application's name is not confidential.","a":["the database password","an API key for a payment service","the application's display name","the private key of a TLS certificate"]},
  "Hacher un mot de passe, c'est… ¦ le transformer de façon irréversible en empreinte, qu'on compare à chaque connexion": {"q":"Hashing a password means…","e":"Hashing is one-way: you cannot recover the password from the hash. At login, the input is hashed and the hashes are compared. Encryption, by contrast, is reversible.","a":["irreversibly turning it into a fingerprint (hash), which is compared at each login","encrypting it with a key so it can be read again later","splitting it into several pieces stored in different places","replacing it with stars on screen"]},
  "Quel fichier, placé à la racine d'un dépôt Git, liste les fichiers à ne pas suivre (par exemple .env qui contient des secrets) ? ¦ .gitignore": {"q":"Which file, placed at the root of a Git repository, lists the files not to track (for example .env, which contains secrets)?","e":"The .gitignore file stops Git from tracking the listed files. Note: it has no effect on a file that has already been committed, which must be removed from tracking and whose secret must be changed.","placeholder":"file name","answers":[".gitignore","gitignore"]},
  "Une faille est signalée dans une bibliothèque de ton projet. Remets la correction dans l'ordre : ¦ Identifier la bibliothèque et la version concernées": {"q":"A flaw is reported in a library used by your project. Put the fix in order:","e":"First you confirm you are affected, update to a fixed version, then test to avoid regressions before deploying.","items":["Identify the affected library and version","Check that a fixed version is available","Update the dependency","Rerun the application's tests","Deploy the new version"]},
  "Quelle est la protection de référence contre l'injection SQL ? ¦ supprimer les apostrophes dans toutes les saisies": {"q":"What is the reference protection against SQL injection?","e":"With a parameterized query, data is sent separately from the SQL code: it can never be interpreted as instructions. Filtering characters or hiding errors is not enough.","a":["removing apostrophes from all inputs","using parameterized (prepared) queries","hiding SQL error messages","limiting field length to 50 characters"]},
  "Quelle mesure empêche le mieux une faille XSS lors de l'affichage d'un commentaire saisi par un utilisateur ? ¦ stocker le commentaire chiffré en base": {"q":"Which measure best prevents an XSS flaw when displaying a comment entered by a user?","e":"Output encoding turns, for example, < into &lt;: the browser displays the text instead of running a script. Modern template engines do this by default, unless it is disabled.","a":["storing the comment encrypted in the database","banning comments longer than 500 characters","encoding (escaping) the content according to the context where it is displayed","checking that the user is logged in"]},
  "Un jeton anti-CSRF doit être imprévisible et lié à la session de l'utilisateur. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"An anti-CSRF token must be unpredictable and tied to the user's session.","e":"The server checks that each sensitive request contains this token. A third-party site cannot guess it: if it were predictable or the same for everyone, it would protect nothing."},
  "Lesquels de ces algorithmes sont adaptés au stockage des mots de passe ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ Argon2id": {"q":"Which of these algorithms are suitable for storing passwords? (several answers)","e":"Argon2id, bcrypt and scrypt are deliberately slow and include a salt. SHA-256 is a good hash function, but too fast: it allows billions of passwords to be tested per second.","a":["Argon2id","SHA-256 alone","bcrypt","scrypt"]},
  "Pour valider un champ « code postal français », quelle approche est la plus sûre ? ¦ refuser les saisies contenant < > ou des guillemets": {"q":"To validate a \"French postal code\" field, which approach is the safest?","e":"An allowlist defines what is permitted and rejects everything else. A denylist always misses a variant that an attacker will eventually find.","a":["reject inputs containing < > or quotation marks","accept everything, then sanitize at display time","reject SQL keywords such as SELECT or DROP","accept exactly 5 digits only"]},
  "Quel attribut de cookie garantit qu'il ne sera envoyé que sur des connexions HTTPS, jamais en HTTP clair ? ¦ Secure": {"q":"Which cookie attribute guarantees it will only be sent over HTTPS connections, never over plain HTTP?","e":"With the Secure attribute, the browser only sends the cookie over HTTPS: it cannot be intercepted on an unencrypted connection. It is combined with HttpOnly and SameSite for a session cookie.","placeholder":"cookie attribute","answers":["Secure","; Secure"]},
  "Le DAST, contrairement au SAST… ¦ teste l'application en fonctionnement, de l'extérieur, sans lire le code": {"q":"DAST, unlike SAST…","e":"DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing) attacks the deployed application like a pentester, for example with OWASP ZAP. SAST reads the code; dependency analysis falls under SCA.","a":["tests the running application from the outside, without reading the code","analyzes the source code and its data flows without ever running it","inventories third-party libraries and their known CVEs","runs on the developer's workstation, in the IDE, before each commit"]},
  "Le sel (salt) ajouté à un mot de passe avant hachage sert surtout à… ¦ rendre le hachage réversible pour l'administrateur": {"q":"The salt added to a password before hashing is mainly used to…","e":"A random salt, unique per user, defeats rainbow tables and prevents two accounts with the same password from having the same hash. It does not remove the need for a slow algorithm.","a":["make the hash reversible for the administrator","make hashes unique and rainbow tables useless","encrypt the hash with a secret key kept off the server","replace a slow algorithm like bcrypt with a faster SHA-256"]},
  "Lesquels de ces outils détectent des secrets (clés, mots de passe) dans un dépôt Git ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ Gitleaks": {"q":"Which of these tools detect secrets (keys, passwords) in a Git repository? (several answers)","e":"Gitleaks, TruffleHog and git-secrets look for secret patterns in the code and history, often in a pre-commit hook or in CI. OWASP ZAP is a DAST scanner for web applications.","a":["Gitleaks","OWASP ZAP","TruffleHog","git-secrets"]},
  "Traitement sûr d'une donnée reçue par une API, dans l'ordre : ¦ Normaliser l'entrée (décodage, forme canonique)": {"q":"Safe handling of data received by an API, in order:","e":"You normalize first so validation applies to the real form (otherwise double encoding can bypass it), then validate, process without concatenation, and encode at display time.","items":["Normalize the input (decoding, canonical form)","Validate the type, length and format","Use the data in a parameterized query","Encode the data on output according to the display context"]},
  "Pourquoi l'attribut de cookie SameSite=Lax limite-t-il les attaques CSRF ? ¦ il chiffre le cookie avec la clé du domaine, illisible pour un site tiers": {"q":"Why does the SameSite=Lax cookie attribute limit CSRF attacks?","e":"With Lax, a POST form submitted from a third-party site is sent without the session cookie. Clicked links (top-level GET) still send it: a sensitive action must therefore never go through a GET. Preventing JavaScript from reading it is the job of HttpOnly.","a":["it encrypts the cookie with the domain's key, unreadable to a third-party site","it limits the cookie's lifetime to the session and deletes it when the browser closes","it is not sent on cross-site requests, except for top-level GET navigations","it prevents JavaScript from reading the cookie, as the HttpOnly attribute does"]},
  "Depuis l'édition 2021 de l'OWASP Top 10, le cross-site scripting (XSS) n'a plus de catégorie propre : il est rangé dans la catégorie « Injection ». ¦ Vrai": {"q":"Since the 2021 edition of the OWASP Top 10, cross-site scripting (XSS) no longer has its own category: it is placed under the \"Injection\" category.","e":"The 2021 edition merged the former XSS category into Injection (A03:2021), and the 2025 edition keeps this grouping (A05:2025). XSS remains data being interpreted as code, here by the browser."},
  "Quelle particularité de bcrypt faut-il connaître ? ¦ il est limité à des mots de passe de 16 caractères et refuse les plus longs": {"q":"Which particularity of bcrypt should you know about?","e":"Beyond 72 bytes, bcrypt silently ignores the rest: two passphrases that differ only after that point are not distinguished. The salt, for its part, is built into the hash.","a":["it is limited to 16-character passwords and rejects longer ones","it does not handle salt, which you have to add yourself","it produces a different hash depending on the processor used","it only takes the first 72 bytes of the password into account"]},
  "Quel algorithme l'OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet recommande-t-il en premier choix pour stocker des mots de passe ? ¦ Argon2id": {"q":"Which algorithm does the OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet recommend as the first choice for storing passwords?","e":"Argon2id, winner of the Password Hashing Competition, is OWASP's first choice. scrypt, bcrypt (for legacy systems) and PBKDF2 (if FIPS compliance is required) remain acceptable.","a":["Argon2id","bcrypt","scrypt","PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256"]},
  "Si tu dois utiliser PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 pour des mots de passe, combien d'itérations l'OWASP recommande-t-elle au minimum ? ¦ 100 000": {"q":"If you must use PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 for passwords, how many iterations does OWASP recommend at a minimum?","e":"OWASP's current recommendation is 600,000 iterations for PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256. The figure of 310,000 matched an earlier version of the cheat sheet.","a":["100,000","310,000","600,000","1,000,000"]},
  "Une requête préparée protège aussi un nom de table ou de colonne passé en paramètre (par exemple pour un tri choisi par l'utilisateur). ¦ Vrai": {"q":"A prepared statement also protects a table or column name passed as a parameter (for example for a sort order chosen by the user).","e":"Parameters only replace values, not SQL identifiers or keywords such as ASC/DESC. For dynamic sorting, you need an allowlist of permitted columns."},
  "Une injection SQL « de second ordre », c'est quand… ¦ l'attaquant enchaîne deux injections dans le même formulaire, la seconde exploitant la première": {"q":"A \"second-order\" SQL injection is when…","e":"The initial insertion is safe, but another part of the code reads the value back and concatenates it into a new query, believing it to be trustworthy. Any data coming from the database must also be parameterized.","a":["the attacker chains two injections in the same form, the second exploiting the first","the payload stored in the database is reused later in a non-parameterized query","the injection targets the secondary replica database rather than the primary one","the attack only succeeds on the second attempt, once the query is cached"]},
  "Pour une XSS basée sur le DOM, lesquels de ces « sinks » JavaScript sont dangereux avec une donnée non fiable ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ element.innerHTML": {"q":"For a DOM-based XSS, which of these JavaScript \"sinks\" are dangerous with untrusted data? (several answers)","e":"innerHTML and document.write() interpret HTML, eval() executes code. textContent inserts plain text: it is the safe alternative for displaying data.","a":["element.innerHTML","element.textContent","document.write()","eval()"]},
  "En Python, pour comparer un jeton secret reçu à sa valeur attendue sans s'exposer à une attaque temporelle, tu utilises… ¦ jeton == attendu": {"q":"In Python, to compare a received secret token with its expected value without exposing yourself to a timing attack, you use…","e":"hmac.compare_digest compares in constant time. The == operator and the all(…) loop stop at the first differing character: the response time gradually reveals the correct value (and zip also ignores a difference in length). secrets.token_hex generates a token; it compares nothing.","a":["jeton == attendu","all(a == b for a, b in zip(jeton, attendu))","secrets.token_hex(jeton)","hmac.compare_digest(jeton, attendu)"]},
  "Déroulé d'une attaque par « dependency confusion », dans l'ordre : ¦ L'attaquant découvre le nom d'un paquet interne privé": {"q":"Sequence of a \"dependency confusion\" attack, in order:","e":"When a project queries both a private registry and the public registry, the highest version may win. Defenses: reserved scopes or namespaces, a single proxy registry, pinned versions.","items":["The attacker discovers the name of a private internal package","They publish a package with the same name and a higher version on the public registry","The misconfigured package manager picks the public, newer version","The malicious code runs at install time, for example via a postinstall script"]},
  "Quel module de la bibliothèque standard Python faut-il utiliser pour générer un jeton de réinitialisation de mot de passe (et non le module random) ? ¦ secrets": {"q":"Which module of the Python standard library should you use to generate a password reset token (and not the random module)?","e":"The secrets module (for example secrets.token_urlsafe()) relies on a cryptographically secure generator. random is predictable: it is meant for simulations, not for security.","placeholder":"module name","answers":["secrets"]},
  "L'IAST se distingue du SAST et du DAST parce qu'il… ¦ analyse uniquement les fichiers de configuration du serveur et du conteneur": {"q":"IAST differs from SAST and DAST in that it…","e":"IAST (Interactive Application Security Testing) places an agent in the application during testing: it sees both the incoming request and the code being executed, which reduces SAST's false positives and pinpoints the faulty line, something DAST cannot do. Blocking attacks in production is the role of RASP.","a":["only analyzes the server and container configuration files","protects the application in production by blocking attacks in real time","instruments the application while it runs to observe it from the inside","compares the compiled code against antivirus signatures"]},
  "Les commandes PowerShell (cmdlets) suivent le format… ¦ Verbe-Nom, comme Get-Process": {"q":"PowerShell commands (cmdlets) follow the format…","e":"Get-, Set-, New-, Remove-… followed by a singular noun. Get-Command lists cmdlets, Get-Help explains how to use them.","a":["Verb-Noun, like Get-Process","Noun-Verb, like Process-Get","FirstName-LastName, like Jean-Michel"]},
  "Dans PowerShell, comment s'écrit une variable ? ¦ %nom%": {"q":"In PowerShell, how do you write a variable?","e":"$name = \"Nova\" creates the variable. %name% is the syntax of the old command prompt (cmd).","a":["%name%","$name","@name"]},
  "À quoi sert le « | » (pipe) dans PowerShell ou Bash ? ¦ À rediriger le résultat d'une commande vers un fichier": {"q":"What is the “|” (pipe) used for in PowerShell or Bash?","e":"Get-Process | Sort-Object CPU sorts processes. In Bash: cat log.txt | grep error. Redirection to a file is “>”, and conditional chaining is “&&”.","a":["To redirect the output of a command to a file","To send the output of one command to the next","To run the next command only if the first one succeeds"]},
  "Quelle première ligne indique qu'un script doit être exécuté par Bash ? ¦ #!/bin/bash": {"q":"Which first line indicates that a script must be run by Bash?","e":"This is the “shebang”: #! followed by the path of the interpreter, /bin/bash. Without the “!”, the line is just a comment. You also need to make the script executable with chmod +x script.sh.","a":["#!/bin/bash","#/bin/bash","#!/bash/bin"]},
  "Trouve le bug : ce script PowerShell affiche toujours « Stock bas ». ¦ $stock = 20": {"q":"Find the bug: this PowerShell script always displays “Low stock”.","e":"In PowerShell, “=” assigns a value: the condition sets stock to 5 and evaluates to true. To compare, write $stock -eq 5 (or -lt 5 for “less than”).","lines":[[{"t":"$stock = 20","c":false}],[{"t":"if (","c":false},{"t":"$stock = 5","c":true},{"t":") {","c":false}],[{"t":"Write-Host \"Low stock\"","c":false}],[{"t":"}","c":false}]]},
  "Remets ce script Bash dans l'ordre : ¦ #!/bin/bash": {"q":"Put this Bash script in order:","e":"The shebang first, then the for … do loop, the commands to repeat, and done to close the loop.","items":["#!/bin/bash","for file in *.log; do","echo \"Analyzing $file\"","done"]},
  "Pour automatiser l'administration de Microsoft 365 en PowerShell aujourd'hui, on utilise surtout… ¦ Le module Microsoft Graph (Connect-MgGraph)": {"q":"To automate Microsoft 365 administration in PowerShell today, you mainly use…","e":"The Microsoft Graph SDK replaces the old MSOnline and AzureAD modules, which Microsoft has now retired. You connect with Connect-MgGraph, requesting only the permissions needed.","a":["The Microsoft Graph module (Connect-MgGraph)","The MSOnline module (Connect-MsolService)","The AzureAD Preview module (Connect-AzureAD)"]},
  "Pourquoi ne jamais écrire un mot de passe en clair dans un script ? ¦ La stratégie d'exécution bloque les scripts qui contiennent un secret": {"q":"Why should you never write a password in clear text in a script?","e":"Use a secrets vault (Azure Key Vault, credential manager), a managed identity or certificate-based authentication.","a":["The execution policy blocks scripts that contain a secret","The script can be read, copied or versioned: the secret leaks","A script containing a secret can no longer be digitally signed"]},
  "Quelle commande affiche l'aide d'une cmdlet PowerShell ? ¦ Get-Command": {"q":"Which command displays the help for a PowerShell cmdlet?","e":"Get-Help Get-Process -Examples shows concrete examples. Get-Command, on the other hand, lists the available cmdlets. The reflex to have before searching the Internet.","a":["Get-Command","Help-Me","Get-Help"]},
  "Un script PowerShell porte l'extension… ¦ .ps1": {"q":"A PowerShell script has the extension…","e":".bat is for the old command prompt, .sh for Bash scripts on Linux.","a":[".ps1",".bat",".sh"]},
  "En Bash, que fait « echo $HOME » ? ¦ Elle va dans le dossier personnel de l'utilisateur": {"q":"In Bash, what does “echo $HOME” do?","e":"$HOME is an environment variable: echo displays its content. To go to that folder, use cd ~ (or cd $HOME).","a":["It goes to the user's home folder","It displays the path of the user's home folder","It displays the text “$HOME” as is"]},
  "La stratégie d'exécution PowerShell (Get-ExecutionPolicy)… ¦ Contrôle quels scripts peuvent s'exécuter, par exemple seulement les scripts signés": {"q":"The PowerShell execution policy (Get-ExecutionPolicy)…","e":"It prevents running a script by mistake, but it is not a real security barrier: an attacker knows how to bypass it. Defense also relies on EDR and PowerShell logging.","a":["Controls which scripts can run, for example only signed scripts","Defines the administrator rights needed to run a script","Blocks scripts deemed malicious by the antivirus before they run"]},
  "Une boucle foreach sert à… ¦ Répéter une action tant qu'une condition est vraie": {"q":"A foreach loop is used to…","e":"foreach ($u in $users) { … } processes accounts one by one: that is the whole point of automation. Repeating while a condition is true is while; choosing between cases is switch.","a":["Repeat an action as long as a condition is true","Choose between several cases based on a value","Repeat an action for each item in a list"]},
  "Afficher les 5 processus qui consomment le plus de mémoire : remets le pipeline dans l'ordre. ¦ Get-Process": {"q":"Display the 5 processes that use the most memory: put the pipeline in order.","e":"You retrieve, sort, then keep the first ones. Each command passes its objects to the next thanks to the pipe.","items":["Get-Process","| Sort-Object WorkingSet -Descending","| Select-Object -First 5"]},
  "Un bloc try / catch dans un script sert à… ¦ Intercepter les erreurs pour les gérer au lieu de laisser le script planter": {"q":"A try / catch block in a script is used to…","e":"In catch, you can write the error to a log, notify the admin or move on to the next item.","a":["Intercept errors to handle them instead of letting the script crash","Test the script without changing anything, like the -WhatIf parameter","Automatically retry a command until it succeeds"]},
  "Pour exporter une liste d'utilisateurs dans un fichier qu'Excel sait ouvrir : ¦ Format-List": {"q":"To export a list of users to a file that Excel can open:","e":"Get-ADUser -Filter * | Select-Object Name, Mail | Export-Csv users.csv -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8: accented characters stay readable. Format-List and Write-Output only display or pass data along, without creating a file.","a":["Format-List","Export-Csv","Write-Output"]},
  "Trouve le bug : ce script Bash n'affiche jamais « Disque presque plein ». ¦ usage=$(df / | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}' | tr -d '%')": {"q":"Find the bug: this Bash script never displays “Disk almost full”.","e":"In Bash, [ is a command: you need spaces around the brackets. Write [ $usage -gt 90 ]. Without the space, Bash displays a “missing ]” error and the condition fails.","lines":[[{"t":"usage=$(df / | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}' | tr -d '%')","c":false}],[{"t":"if [ $usage -gt ","c":false},{"t":"90]","c":true},{"t":"; then","c":false}],[{"t":"echo \"Disk almost full\"","c":false}],[{"t":"fi","c":false}]]},
  "Pour qu'un script planifié se connecte à Microsoft Graph sans mot de passe stocké, on utilise plutôt… ¦ Un compte de service dont le mot de passe est chiffré avec ConvertFrom-SecureString": {"q":"For a scheduled script to connect to Microsoft Graph without a stored password, you would rather use…","e":"An encrypted password is still a stored password, and a copied token expires after about an hour. You give the application only the Graph permissions it needs, and the certificate is protected on the machine that runs the script.","a":["A service account whose password is encrypted with ConvertFrom-SecureString","An Entra ID application with a certificate, or a managed identity","An access token copied from Graph Explorer and pasted into the script"]},
  "Quel opérateur PowerShell teste l'égalité entre deux valeurs ? ¦ -eq": {"q":"Which PowerShell operator tests equality between two values?","e":"-eq (equal), -ne (not equal), -lt / -gt (less than / greater than). The = sign is used only to assign a value.","placeholder":"Operator","answers":["-eq"]},
  "Quelle première ligne (le « shebang ») indique qu'un script doit être exécuté par Bash ? ¦ #!/bin/bash": {"q":"Which first line (the “shebang”) indicates that a script must be run by Bash?","e":"#!/usr/bin/env bash is a more portable variant, which looks for Bash wherever it is installed.","placeholder":"#!…","answers":["#!/bin/bash","#!/usr/bin/env bash","#! /bin/bash"]},
  "Quelle cmdlet PowerShell liste les services Windows et leur état (démarré, arrêté) ? ¦ Get-Service": {"q":"Which PowerShell cmdlet lists Windows services and their state (started, stopped)?","e":"Get-Service displays services; Start-Service, Stop-Service and Restart-Service let you act on them. Same Verb-Noun logic as everywhere in PowerShell.","placeholder":"Verb-Noun…","answers":["Get-Service"]},
  "En PowerShell comme en Bash, quel caractère commence un commentaire sur une ligne ? ¦ //": {"q":"In PowerShell as in Bash, which character starts a single-line comment?","e":"Everything after # is ignored at runtime. “//” is used in JavaScript or C, “--” in SQL.","a":["//","--","#"]},
  "Dans Get-Service | Where-Object { $_.Status -eq \"Running\" }, que représente $_ ? ¦ Le résultat complet renvoyé par Get-Service": {"q":"In Get-Service | Where-Object { $_.Status -eq \"Running\" }, what does $_ represent?","e":"$_ (or $PSItem) refers to the current item: here, each service received in turn. You can therefore read its properties, like $_.Status or $_.Name.","a":["The complete result returned by Get-Service","The object currently being processed in the pipeline","The current folder, as with Get-Location"]},
  "En Bash, on affecte une variable sans espace autour du signe égal : nom=\"Alice\". ¦ Vrai": {"q":"In Bash, you assign a variable with no spaces around the equals sign: name=\"Alice\".","e":"With spaces (name = \"Alice\"), Bash thinks “name” is a command and fails. You then read the variable back with $name."},
  "En Bash, que contient la variable spéciale $? juste après l'exécution d'une commande ? ¦ Le code de sortie de la dernière commande": {"q":"In Bash, what does the special variable $? contain right after a command has run?","e":"0 means success, any other value means failure. $! gives the PID of the last background process and $# the number of arguments.","a":["The exit code of the last command","The PID of the last process started in the background","The number of arguments passed to the script"]},
  "Gestion d'erreurs en PowerShell : remets les blocs dans l'ordre. ¦ try { Copy-Item $source $dest -ErrorAction Stop }": {"q":"Error handling in PowerShell: put the blocks in the right order.","e":"try contains the risky code, catch handles the error, and finally runs in every case (cleanup, closing a connection). -ErrorAction Stop makes the error terminating so that it can be caught.","items":["try { Copy-Item $source $dest -ErrorAction Stop }","catch { Write-Warning $_.Exception.Message }","finally { Write-Host \"Copy finished\" }"]},
  "Que provoque Set-StrictMode -Version Latest en tête d'un script PowerShell ? ¦ L'exécution des seuls scripts signés numériquement": {"q":"What does Set-StrictMode -Version Latest do at the top of a PowerShell script?","e":"Strict mode catches typos ($usre instead of $user), nonexistent properties and out-of-range indexes. Stopping on errors, on the other hand, is set with $ErrorActionPreference = \"Stop\".","a":["It runs only digitally signed scripts","It stops the script at the first non-terminating error","It raises an error when an uninitialized variable is read"]},
  "Pourquoi ce pipeline ne trie-t-il pas les fichiers comme prévu ?\nGet-ChildItem | Format-Table Name | Sort-Object Name ¦ Get-ChildItem renvoie du texte brut, que Sort-Object ne sait pas trier par nom": {"q":"Why does this pipeline not sort the files as expected?\nGet-ChildItem | Format-Table Name | Sort-Object Name","e":"PowerShell passes objects through the pipeline. The Format-* cmdlets turn them into display objects that no longer have a Name property: sort and filter first, format last.","a":["Get-ChildItem returns plain text, which Sort-Object cannot sort by name","Format-Table emits formatting objects, not files anymore: you format at the end of the pipeline","You must write Sort-Object -Property $Name, with the $ in front of the property"]},
  "En Bash, quel opérateur envoie la sortie d'une commande dans un fichier en écrasant son contenu ? ¦ >": {"q":"In Bash, which operator sends a command's output to a file, overwriting its contents?","e":"“>” redirects standard output to a file and overwrites it if it exists. “|” sends the output to another command, “<” reads input from a file.","a":[">","|","<","&"]},
  "En Bash, « commande >> journal.txt » ajoute la sortie à la fin du fichier sans effacer son contenu. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"In Bash, “command >> journal.txt” appends the output to the end of the file without erasing its contents.","e":"“>>” appends to the end of the file (and creates it if it does not exist), unlike “>” which overwrites. Perfect for keeping a log."},
  "Quelle cmdlet PowerShell liste le contenu d'un dossier ? ¦ Get-ChildItem": {"q":"Which PowerShell cmdlet lists the contents of a folder?","e":"Get-ChildItem lists files and subfolders. ls, dir and gci are aliases for it on Windows.","placeholder":"Verb-Noun","answers":["Get-ChildItem","gci","ls","dir"]},
  "Sous Linux, quel outil permet de lancer un script automatiquement chaque nuit ? ¦ grep": {"q":"On Linux, which tool lets you run a script automatically every night?","e":"cron runs commands on a schedule defined in a crontab. grep searches text, chmod changes permissions and ssh opens a remote connection.","a":["grep","chmod","cron","ssh"]},
  "Sous Windows, quel outil permet de lancer un script automatiquement à heure fixe ? ¦ Le Gestionnaire des tâches": {"q":"On Windows, which tool lets you run a script automatically at a set time?","e":"Task Scheduler triggers a program or script on a schedule or on an event (startup, sign-in…). Task Manager, on the other hand, shows what is running right now.","a":["Task Manager","Event Viewer","Resource Monitor","Task Scheduler"]},
  "Quelle cmdlet PowerShell lit le contenu d'un fichier texte ? ¦ Get-Content": {"q":"Which PowerShell cmdlet reads the contents of a text file?","e":"Get-Content reads a file line by line. Read-Host reads what the user types on the keyboard and Get-Item returns the file object (size, dates), not its contents.","a":["Get-Content","Read-Host","Get-Item","Import-Text"]},
  "Quels sont les avantages d'un script pour une tâche répétitive ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ Gagner du temps": {"q":"What are the advantages of a script for a repetitive task? (several answers)","e":"A script saves time and repeats the same actions without typos. However, you must always test it, ideally on a limited scope, before using it for real.","a":["Saving time","Reducing manual typing errors","No longer needing to test","Doing exactly the same thing every time"]},
  "Que fait ce code Bash ?\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo $i; done ¦ Il affiche « 1 2 3 » sur une seule ligne": {"q":"What does this Bash code do?\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo $i; done","e":"The for loop successively gives i the values 1, 2 and 3, and echo prints each value followed by a line break.","a":["It prints “1 2 3” on a single line","It prints 1, 2 then 3, each on its own line","It prints “$i” three times","It prints only 3"]},
  "Que signifie la ligne de crontab suivante ?\n30 2 * * * /opt/scripts/sauvegarde.sh ¦ Toutes les 30 minutes, 2 fois par jour": {"q":"What does the following crontab line mean?\n30 2 * * * /opt/scripts/sauvegarde.sh","e":"The 5 fields are: minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week. “30 2 * * *” therefore means 2:30, every day.","a":["Every 30 minutes, 2 times a day","On the 2nd of every month at 0:30","Every day at 2:30","Every 2 hours, at minute 30"]},
  "En Bash, quelle commande rend le script sauvegarde.sh exécutable ? ¦ chmod +x sauvegarde.sh": {"q":"In Bash, which command makes the script sauvegarde.sh executable?","e":"chmod +x adds the execute permission; you can then run the script with ./sauvegarde.sh.","placeholder":"chmod …","answers":["chmod +x sauvegarde.sh","chmod u+x sauvegarde.sh","chmod 755 sauvegarde.sh","chmod 744 sauvegarde.sh","chmod a+x sauvegarde.sh","chmod +x ./sauvegarde.sh","chmod ugo+x sauvegarde.sh","chmod 700 sauvegarde.sh","chmod 750 sauvegarde.sh","chmod 775 sauvegarde.sh"]},
  "En PowerShell, Write-Host envoie ses données dans le pipeline, exactement comme Write-Output. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"In PowerShell, Write-Host sends its data into the pipeline, exactly like Write-Output.","e":"Write-Host writes directly to the screen (to the information stream, no. 6), not to the pipeline: its text does not reach a variable or the next command, unless explicitly redirected (6>&1). Write-Output, on the other hand, sends objects into the pipeline."},
  "En Bash, que redirige « 2> erreurs.txt » ? ¦ La sortie standard": {"q":"In Bash, what does “2> erreurs.txt” redirect?","e":"Descriptor 1 is standard output (stdout) and 2 is error output (stderr). “2>” therefore sends only error messages to the file.","a":["Standard output","Error output (stderr)","The first two lines of the output","Standard input"]},
  "En Bash, que fait « ./script.sh > log.txt 2>&1 » ? ¦ Envoie seulement les erreurs dans log.txt": {"q":"In Bash, what does “./script.sh > log.txt 2>&1” do?","e":"“> log.txt” redirects stdout to the file, then “2>&1” redirects stderr to the same destination as stdout. Order matters: “2>&1 > log.txt” would leave the errors on the screen.","a":["Sends only the errors to log.txt","Sends the output to log.txt and the errors to a file named “1”","Sends both standard output and errors to log.txt","Appends the output to the end of log.txt without overwriting it"]},
  "Ce bloc n'entre jamais dans le catch quand le fichier n'existe pas. Pourquoi ?\ntry { Get-Item C:\\absent.txt } catch { Write-Output \"Erreur\" } ¦ Get-Item renvoie $null sans erreur quand le fichier est absent": {"q":"This block never enters the catch when the file does not exist. Why?\ntry { Get-Item C:\\absent.txt } catch { Write-Output \"Error\" }","e":"try/catch only intercepts terminating errors. With -ErrorAction Stop, Get-Item's non-terminating error becomes terminating and the catch runs.","a":["Get-Item returns $null without an error when the file is missing","A finally block is needed for the catch to be taken into account","The catch must specify the type [System.IO.FileNotFoundException]","Get-Item produces a non-terminating error: you need to add -ErrorAction Stop"]},
  "Remets cette boucle Bash qui affiche un fichier ligne par ligne dans l'ordre : ¦ while IFS= read -r ligne": {"q":"Put this Bash loop that prints a file line by line in the right order:","e":"The while loop reads one line per iteration; the “< fichier.txt” redirection goes after done. -r prevents backslash interpretation and IFS= keeps leading spaces on the line.","items":["while IFS= read -r ligne","do","echo \"$ligne\"","done < fichier.txt"]},
  "En Bash, quelles lignes testent correctement si le fichier /etc/app.conf existe ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ [ -f /etc/app.conf ]": {"q":"In Bash, which lines correctly test whether the file /etc/app.conf exists? (several answers)","e":"[ ] and test are equivalent, and [[ ]] is Bash's extended version. The spaces after “[” and before “]” are mandatory: “[-f” is read as a nonexistent command.","a":["[ -f /etc/app.conf ]","test -f /etc/app.conf","[-f /etc/app.conf]","[[ -e /etc/app.conf ]]"]},
  "Quel paramètre commun PowerShell montre ce qu'une commande ferait, sans l'exécuter réellement ? ¦ -WhatIf": {"q":"Which common PowerShell parameter shows what a command would do, without actually running it?","e":"-WhatIf simulates the action (e.g. Remove-Item *.log -WhatIf) and displays what would be done. Essential before a bulk deletion.","placeholder":"-…","answers":["-WhatIf","WhatIf"]},
  "En Bash, quel est l'effet de « set -o pipefail » ? ¦ Le script s'arrête dès qu'une commande du pipeline échoue": {"q":"In Bash, what is the effect of “set -o pipefail”?","e":"Without pipefail, “failing_cmd | tee log” returns tee's exit code, so 0. With pipefail, the pipeline returns the exit code of the rightmost command that failed; it is set -e that makes the script stop.","a":["The script stops as soon as a command in the pipeline fails","The pipeline returns the exit code of the last failing command","Pipeline errors are redirected to a log file","Pipelines are run in parallel, which is faster"]},
  "Quel est l'effet de « $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop' » en tête d'un script PowerShell ? ¦ Le script s'arrête dès qu'une variable non définie est utilisée": {"q":"What is the effect of “$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'” at the top of a PowerShell script?","e":"This preference variable sets the default behavior of -ErrorAction for cmdlets. With Stop, every error halts execution or triggers the catch of a try/catch. Undefined variables are the job of Set-StrictMode.","a":["The script stops as soon as an undefined variable is used","Errors are hidden and the script continues without displaying them","Non-terminating errors become terminating, and so can be caught","The script asks for confirmation before each command that changes the system"]},
  "Pourquoi « for f in $(ls *.txt); do … » est-il une mauvaise pratique en Bash ? ¦ Parce que $(…) relance ls à chaque tour de boucle, ce qui ralentit fortement le script": {"q":"Why is “for f in $(ls *.txt); do …” bad practice in Bash?","e":"$(ls) is run only once, but its output undergoes word splitting: “my report.txt” becomes two items. The *.txt glob provides each file name intact.","a":["Because $(…) runs ls again on every loop iteration, which slows the script down a lot","Because $(ls) only returns the first page of results in a large folder","Because ls truncates file names longer than 255 characters in a substitution","Because names with spaces are split into several words; “for f in *.txt” is safer"]},
  "Dans une crontab, le champ « jour de la semaine » accepte aussi bien 0 que 7 pour dimanche. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"In a crontab, the “day of week” field accepts both 0 and 7 for Sunday.","e":"In common cron implementations (Vixie cron, cronie), 0 and 7 both mean Sunday. You can also use abbreviated English names, such as sun."},
  "Le paramètre -Parallel de ForEach-Object est disponible… ¦ À partir de PowerShell 7.0": {"q":"The -Parallel parameter of ForEach-Object is available…","e":"ForEach-Object -Parallel appeared with PowerShell 7.0 and does not exist in Windows PowerShell 5.1. Each iteration runs in its own runspace: outer variables are passed with $using:.","a":["From PowerShell 7.0 onward","In Windows PowerShell 5.1 and later","Only in PowerShell ISE","Only with the ThreadJob module installed"]},
  "En PowerShell, avec $nom = 'Alice', quelles instructions affichent « Bonjour Alice » ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ \"Bonjour $nom\"": {"q":"In PowerShell, with $name = 'Alice', which statements display “Hello Alice”? (several answers)","e":"Double quotes expand variables and $(…) subexpressions; concatenation with + also works. Inside single quotes, $name stays literal text.","a":["\"Hello $name\"","'Hello ' + $name","'Hello $name'","\"Hello $($name)\""]},
  "En Bash, quelle variable spéciale contient le PID du dernier processus lancé en arrière-plan (avec &) ? ¦ $!": {"q":"In Bash, which special variable contains the PID of the last process started in the background (with &)?","e":"$! gives the PID of the last command started in the background: handy for waiting for it with wait \"$!\" or stopping it with kill. Not to be confused with $$ (PID of the current shell) or $? (exit code).","placeholder":"$…","answers":["$!"]},
  "Dans une page web, quel est le rôle du CSS ? ¦ Définir la structure : titres, paragraphes, listes": {"q":"In a web page, what is the role of CSS?","e":"HTML for structure, CSS for style, JavaScript for interactivity. Power Up is built exactly like that!","a":["Defining the structure: headings, paragraphs, lists","Handling appearance: colors, fonts, layout","Processing data: calculations, forms, submissions"]},
  "Le code HTTP 404 signifie… ¦ Page introuvable": {"q":"HTTP code 404 means…","e":"4xx codes are client-side errors (404: not found), 5xx are server-side errors (500: internal error), and 200 means everything is fine.","a":["Page not found","Server down","The support desk's room number"]},
  "Quelle différence entre les codes HTTP 401 et 403 ? ¦ 401 : authentifié mais pas autorisé ; 403 : pas authentifié": {"q":"What is the difference between HTTP codes 401 and 403?","e":"401 = “who are you?”, you need to sign in. 403 = “I know who you are, but you're not allowed”.","a":["401: authenticated but not authorized; 403: not authenticated","401: not authenticated; 403: authenticated but not authorized","401: malformed request; 403: too many requests"]},
  "Dans une API REST, quelle méthode HTTP sert en général à créer une ressource ? ¦ HEAD": {"q":"In a REST API, which HTTP method is generally used to create a resource?","e":"GET reads, POST creates, PUT/PATCH modifies, DELETE deletes. HEAD only returns the headers and OPTIONS lists the allowed methods. The data exchanged is most often in JSON format.","a":["HEAD","POST","OPTIONS"]},
  "Une clé d'API peut être laissée dans le code JavaScript d'une page publique. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"An API key can be left in the JavaScript code of a public page.","e":"False: anyone can read the code of a web page. A secret key stays on the server side, and you change it immediately if it has leaked."},
  "HTTPS, c'est… ¦ HTTP compressé avec gzip": {"q":"HTTPS is…","e":"TLS encrypts the traffic and the certificate proves the server's identity. But remember: the padlock doesn't guarantee that a site is honest!","a":["HTTP compressed with gzip","HTTP encrypted with TLS","HTTP protected by a password"]},
  "Qu'est-ce que l'OWASP Top 10 ? ¦ Un classement annuel des dix vulnérabilités CVE les plus exploitées": {"q":"What is the OWASP Top 10?","e":"Broken access control, injections, misconfigurations… It's the basic checklist for any security-minded web developer.","a":["An annual ranking of the ten most exploited CVE vulnerabilities","A reference list of the most critical risks for web applications","A mandatory certification standard for public web applications"]},
  "Dans une page web, le JavaScript sert à… ¦ Rendre la page interactive : clics, menus, contenus mis à jour": {"q":"In a web page, JavaScript is used to…","e":"Power Up's animated tabs and drag-and-drop are built with JavaScript.","a":["Make the page interactive: clicks, menus, updated content","Define the structure: headings, paragraphs, images","Make coffee while the page loads"]},
  "Une URL, c'est… ¦ Un langage de programmation pour créer des sites": {"q":"A URL is…","e":"It contains the protocol (https), the domain name (www.exemple.fr) and the path (/contact). It's also the first thing to check when faced with a suspicious link.","a":["A programming language for building websites","The address of a resource on the web, such as https://www.exemple.fr/contact","A “User Remaining Logged-in”, in support jargon"]},
  "N'importe quel visiteur peut consulter le code HTML d'une page publique. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"Any visitor can view the HTML code of a public page.","e":"True: press Ctrl + U in the browser. Hence the rule: no secrets in client-side code."},
  "Le format JSON sert à… ¦ Décrire la mise en page d'un site web, à la manière du CSS": {"q":"The JSON format is used to…","e":"Example: {\"name\": \"Nyx\", \"xp\": 120}. Power Up also saves your progress as JSON in your browser.","a":["Describe the layout of a website, like CSS","Run code on the server side, like PHP or Python","Exchange structured data as readable text, through APIs"]},
  "Un cookie, c'est… ¦ Une petite donnée enregistrée par le navigateur pour un site : session, préférences…": {"q":"A cookie is…","e":"Session cookies are as good as a login: they must be protected (HttpOnly, Secure). Advertising cookies require the user's consent. The local copy of a site's files is the cache.","a":["A small piece of data saved by the browser for a site: session, preferences…","A local copy of a site's images and scripts so it displays faster","A virtual biscuit given to the most loyal visitors"]},
  "Une redirection permanente d'une page vers une autre renvoie le code HTTP… ¦ 302": {"q":"A permanent redirect from one page to another returns the HTTP code…","e":"301: moved permanently. 302: temporary redirect. And 418 “I'm a teapot” is a joke from 1998 that is still famous among developers!","a":["302","301","418"]},
  "Pourquoi une API utilise-t-elle un jeton d'accès plutôt que le mot de passe de l'utilisateur ? ¦ Le jeton ne peut pas être volé ni rejoué, contrairement au mot de passe": {"q":"Why does an API use an access token rather than the user's password?","e":"This is the principle of OAuth 2.0, used by Microsoft Graph: the application receives a token with only the permissions granted, which can be revoked without changing the password. A stolen token remains usable until it expires: HTTPS is still essential.","a":["The token can't be stolen or replayed, unlike the password","The token is limited in duration and permissions, and can be revoked separately","The token allows calling the API over plain HTTP, with no need for TLS"]},
  "Le mécanisme CORS sert à… ¦ Empêcher un autre site d'afficher tes pages dans une iframe": {"q":"The CORS mechanism is used to…","e":"By default, the browser blocks reading responses coming from another domain; the API server states which ones it allows. Displaying in an iframe is governed by X-Frame-Options, and injected scripts by CSP.","a":["Prevent another site from displaying your pages in an iframe","Block the execution of scripts injected into the web page","Allow other sites to call an API from a browser"]},
  "Quand tu ouvres un site en HTTPS, dans l'ordre : ¦ Le DNS traduit le nom de domaine en adresse IP": {"q":"When you open a site over HTTPS, in order:","e":"A classic interview question! Every step can be the source of an outage: DNS, network, certificate, server…","items":["DNS translates the domain name into an IP address","The browser opens a TCP connection with the server","The TLS handshake encrypts the connection","The browser sends the HTTP request and receives the response","The browser displays the page"]},
  "Une API REST est dite « sans état » (stateless) : ¦ Chaque requête contient tout le nécessaire ; le serveur ne garde pas de contexte entre deux appels": {"q":"A REST API is said to be “stateless”:","e":"That's why the authentication token is sent again with every call. This principle makes it easier to scale across several servers.","a":["Each request contains everything needed; the server keeps no context between two calls","It stores no persistent data: no database behind the API","The server keeps a session per client so the client doesn't have to resend its token"]},
  "Quel code HTTP signifie « authentifié, mais pas autorisé » ? ¦ 403": {"q":"Which HTTP code means “authenticated, but not authorized”?","e":"401: you need to authenticate. 403: we know who you are, but you don't have the right.","placeholder":"Code","answers":["403"]},
  "Quelle balise HTML crée un lien cliquable vers une autre page ? ¦ <link>": {"q":"Which HTML tag creates a clickable link to another page?","e":"<a href=\"https://exemple.fr\">text</a> creates a link. href is an attribute, and <link> is used to attach a CSS stylesheet to the page.","a":["<link>","<href>","<a>"]},
  "En JSON, on peut ajouter des commentaires avec // comme en JavaScript. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"In JSON, you can add comments with // as in JavaScript.","e":"Standard JSON doesn't accept any comments: a strict parser will return an error. Keys must also be enclosed in double quotes."},
  "Quel code HTTP renvoie un serveur quand une requête a réussi normalement ? ¦ 200": {"q":"Which HTTP code does a server return when a request succeeded normally?","e":"200 OK = success. 2xx codes indicate success (201 Created after a creation), 3xx a redirect, 4xx a client error and 5xx a server error.","placeholder":"3-digit code","answers":["200","200 OK"]},
  "Un code HTTP de la famille 5xx (500, 502, 503…) indique… ¦ Une erreur côté serveur": {"q":"An HTTP code in the 5xx family (500, 502, 503…) indicates…","e":"500 = internal error, 502 = bad gateway (the server behind a proxy isn't responding correctly), 503 = service unavailable. From a support perspective, it's a sign that the problem doesn't come from the user's device.","a":["A server-side error","A client-side error (malformed request)","A redirect to another address"]},
  "Ton script appelle une API en boucle et reçoit soudain le code 429. Que se passe-t-il ? ¦ La ressource a été déplacée définitivement vers une autre URL": {"q":"Your script calls an API in a loop and suddenly gets code 429. What is happening?","e":"429 Too Many Requests signals throttling. You need to slow down, respect the Retry-After header if present, and retry with an increasing delay. An expired token would rather give 401, a moved resource 301.","a":["The resource has been permanently moved to another URL","Too many requests: you are exceeding the rate limit","Your access token has expired and must be renewed"]},
  "Structure d'une requête HTTP/1.1 : remets les parties dans l'ordre. ¦ La ligne de requête (méthode, chemin, version)": {"q":"Structure of an HTTP/1.1 request: put the parts in order.","e":"Example: “POST /api/tickets HTTP/1.1”, then the headers, an empty line marking their end, and finally the body, for example JSON. A GET generally has no body.","items":["The request line (method, path, version)","The headers (Host, Authorization, Content-Type…)","An empty line","The body, optional"]},
  "Selon la spécification HTTP (RFC 9110), laquelle de ces méthodes n'est PAS idempotente ? ¦ DELETE": {"q":"According to the HTTP specification (RFC 9110), which of these methods is NOT idempotent?","e":"Repeating a PUT or a DELETE leaves the resource in the same final state; repeating a POST can create several resources. PATCH is not guaranteed to be idempotent either (e.g. “add 1 to the counter”).","a":["DELETE","PUT","POST"]},
  "Depuis https://app.exemple.fr, quelles requêtes fetch vers https://api.autre.fr déclenchent une requête préliminaire CORS (preflight OPTIONS) ? ¦ Un GET simple, sans en-tête personnalisé": {"q":"From https://app.exemple.fr, which fetch requests to https://api.autre.fr trigger a CORS preflight request (OPTIONS preflight)?","e":"The preflight is avoided only for “simple” requests: GET, HEAD or POST with allowed headers and a form or text/plain Content-Type. Other methods, application/json and the Authorization header trigger a preliminary OPTIONS request.","a":["A simple GET, with no custom header","A DELETE","A POST with Content-Type: application/json","A GET with an Authorization: Bearer … header"]},
  "Que signifie le sigle HTML ? ¦ High Transfer Machine Language": {"q":"What does the acronym HTML stand for?","e":"HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is a markup language: it describes the structure and content of a page (headings, paragraphs, links, images). It is not a programming language.","a":["High Transfer Machine Language","Hyperlink Text Management Language","HyperText Markup Language","Home Tool Markup Language"]},
  "Le cadenas affiché à côté de l'adresse garantit que le site est honnête et sans danger. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"The padlock displayed next to the address guarantees that the site is honest and safe.","e":"The padlock only indicates that the connection is encrypted (HTTPS) with a certificate valid for that domain name. Many phishing sites also have a padlock: you must always check the exact address."},
  "Tu tapes une adresse dans le navigateur et tu valides. Quelle méthode HTTP le navigateur utilise-t-il pour récupérer la page ? ¦ GET": {"q":"You type an address in the browser and press Enter. Which HTTP method does the browser use to retrieve the page?","e":"GET is used to read a resource without modifying it: it's the method used to display a page. POST is rather used to send data, for example a form.","a":["GET","POST","PUT","DELETE"]},
  "Quelle touche du clavier ouvre les outils de développement dans Chrome, Edge ou Firefox sous Windows ? ¦ F12": {"q":"Which keyboard key opens the developer tools in Chrome, Edge or Firefox on Windows?","e":"F12 opens the developer tools (DevTools): HTML/CSS inspection, JavaScript console, Network tab to see HTTP requests and their codes. Ctrl+Shift+I does the same thing.","placeholder":"A key…","answers":["F12"]},
  "Un utilisateur voit encore l'ancienne version d'une page mise à jour. Vider le cache du navigateur sert à… ¦ Supprimer son historique de mots de passe enregistrés": {"q":"A user still sees the old version of an updated page. Clearing the browser cache is used to…","e":"The cache keeps a local copy of images, CSS and scripts to load pages faster. If it is stale, the browser displays old versions; clearing it (or pressing Ctrl+F5) forces a new download.","a":["Delete their saved password history","Speed up their Internet connection by freeing up bandwidth","Reset the browser's extensions and settings","Force a re-download of the site files kept as a local copy"]},
  "Lesquels sont des navigateurs web ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ Apache": {"q":"Which of these are web browsers? (several answers)","e":"Firefox, Safari and Edge are browsers: they display pages on the user side. Apache is a web server: it hosts sites and responds to browsers' requests.","a":["Apache","Firefox","Safari","Edge"]},
  "Quel en-tête HTTP indique le format du contenu envoyé ou renvoyé (par exemple application/json) ? ¦ Content-Type": {"q":"Which HTTP header indicates the format of the content sent or returned (for example application/json)?","e":"Content-Type describes the type of the message body (text/html, application/json…). Accept, on the other hand, is sent by the client to say which formats it wants to receive in response.","a":["Content-Type","Accept","Content-Length","Authorization"]},
  "Un POST sur /api/tickets vient de créer un nouveau ticket. Quel code de réponse est le plus approprié ? ¦ 204 No Content": {"q":"A POST to /api/tickets has just created a new ticket. Which response code is the most appropriate?","e":"201 Created signals that a resource has been created, often with a Location header giving its address. 202 only means that the request has been accepted for later processing.","a":["204 No Content","302 Found","201 Created","202 Accepted"]},
  "Le contenu (payload) d'un jeton JWT signé est illisible pour qui ne possède pas la clé secrète. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"The content (payload) of a signed JWT is unreadable for anyone who doesn't have the secret key.","e":"A signed JWT is simply Base64URL-encoded: anyone can read its content (jwt.io, for example). The signature only guarantees that it hasn't been modified; so you never put secrets in it."},
  "Remets ces éléments d'un document HTML dans leur ordre d'apparition : ¦ <!DOCTYPE html>": {"q":"Put these elements of an HTML document in the order they appear:","e":"The DOCTYPE comes first, then the <html> tag that wraps everything. <head> contains the metadata, including <title>, and <body> then contains the visible content of the page.","items":["<!DOCTYPE html>","<html>","<head>","<title>","<body>"]},
  "Quel attribut de cookie empêche le JavaScript de la page de le lire via document.cookie ? ¦ HttpOnly": {"q":"Which cookie attribute prevents the page's JavaScript from reading it via document.cookie?","e":"An HttpOnly cookie is only sent in HTTP requests and remains invisible to JavaScript. It is an essential protection for session cookies against theft through an XSS flaw.","placeholder":"Attribute name…","answers":["HttpOnly","http only","http-only"]},
  "Quel flux OAuth 2.0 est recommandé en 2026 pour une application monopage (SPA) ou une application mobile ? ¦ Implicit (jeton renvoyé directement dans l'URL)": {"q":"Which OAuth 2.0 flow is recommended in 2026 for a single-page application (SPA) or a mobile application?","e":"Authorization Code with PKCE protects the code exchange even for a public client that can't keep a secret. The Implicit and Password flows are discouraged by OAuth security best practices; Client Credentials is used for server-to-server exchanges, with no user.","a":["Implicit (token returned directly in the URL)","Resource Owner Password Credentials","Client Credentials","Authorization Code with PKCE"]},
  "Un cookie de session porte l'attribut SameSite=Strict. Que se passe-t-il ? ¦ Il n'est envoyé que sur des connexions HTTPS chiffrées": {"q":"A session cookie has the SameSite=Strict attribute. What happens?","e":"SameSite=Strict blocks the cookie in any cross-site request, including navigation via a link: the user then arrives logged out. Sending over HTTPS only corresponds to Secure and invisibility to JavaScript to HttpOnly.","a":["It is only sent over encrypted HTTPS connections","It is deleted as soon as the tab or browser is closed","It is not sent if the request comes from another site, even via a link","It is unreadable for JavaScript, via document.cookie for example"]},
  "Quelles parties composent un JWT signé au format compact (séparées par des points) ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ L'en-tête (header)": {"q":"Which parts make up a signed JWT in compact format (separated by dots)? (several answers)","e":"A signed JWT (JWS) has three Base64URL parts: header.payload.signature. The initialization vector only exists in an encrypted JWT (JWE), which has five parts.","a":["The header","The payload","The initialization vector","The signature"]},
  "Quel en-tête de réponse empêche le navigateur de « deviner » le type d'un fichier (MIME sniffing), par exemple d'exécuter comme script un fichier servi en text/plain ? ¦ X-Frame-Options": {"q":"Which response header prevents the browser from “guessing” a file's type (MIME sniffing), for example executing as a script a file served as text/plain?","e":"X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff forces the browser to respect the declared Content-Type. X-Frame-Options controls display in an iframe (clickjacking), Referrer-Policy the Referer header, and Content-Disposition whether a file is downloaded or displayed.","a":["X-Frame-Options","Referrer-Policy","X-Content-Type-Options","Content-Disposition"]},
  "Si ton API n'autorise pas une origine dans ses en-têtes CORS, un script curl ou Python ne pourra pas l'appeler non plus. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"If your API doesn't allow an origin in its CORS headers, a curl or Python script won't be able to call it either.","e":"CORS is a rule enforced by the browser, based on the headers returned by the server: curl, Python or Postman ignore them completely. An API must therefore always be protected by real authentication."},
  "Le navigateur envoie une requête avec If-None-Match et la ressource n'a pas changé. Quel code HTTP le serveur renvoie-t-il ? ¦ 304": {"q":"The browser sends a request with If-None-Match and the resource hasn't changed. Which HTTP code does the server return?","e":"304 Not Modified indicates that the cached version (identified by its ETag) is still valid: the server sends no body and the browser reuses its local copy.","placeholder":"3-digit code…","answers":["304","304 Not Modified"]},
  "Quelle est la différence entre localStorage et sessionStorage ? ¦ localStorage est chiffré, sessionStorage ne l'est pas": {"q":"What is the difference between localStorage and sessionStorage?","e":"Both store key/value pairs per origin without ever sending them to the server (unlike cookies). sessionStorage is limited to the tab and disappears when it is closed; localStorage remains until deleted.","a":["localStorage is encrypted, sessionStorage is not","sessionStorage is sent to the server with every request, localStorage is not","sessionStorage is erased when the tab is closed, localStorage persists","None, they are two names for the same API"]},
  "Quel élément OpenID Connect ajoute-t-il à OAuth 2.0 pour authentifier l'utilisateur ? ¦ Le refresh token, qui prolonge la session": {"q":"What element does OpenID Connect add to OAuth 2.0 to authenticate the user?","e":"OAuth 2.0 is a delegated authorization protocol: the access token gives access to an API but is not intended for the client to identify the user. OpenID Connect adds the ID token (signed JWT with sub, iss, aud, exp…) and the openid scope.","a":["The refresh token, which extends the session","The authorization code, exchanged for a token","The offline_access scope, for offline access","The ID token, a signed JWT describing the user"]},
  "Une API qui renvoie Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true peut répondre Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * pour accepter les requêtes avec cookies depuis toutes les origines. ¦ Vrai": {"q":"An API that returns Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true can respond with Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * to accept requests with cookies from all origins.","e":"For a request with credentials (cookies, credentials: 'include'), the browser rejects the response if Access-Control-Allow-Origin is *. The server must return the exact origin (and ideally Vary: Origin)."},
  "Quelle revendication (claim) d'un JWT faut-il vérifier pour s'assurer que le jeton a bien été émis pour TON API, et pas pour une autre application ? ¦ iss": {"q":"Which claim of a JWT must you verify to make sure the token was issued for YOUR API, and not for another application?","e":"aud (audience) designates the intended recipient of the token: without this check, a valid token issued for another application of the same provider would be accepted. iss identifies the issuer, sub the user and jti the token's unique identifier (useful against replay).","a":["iss","sub","jti","aud"]},
  "Selon la RFC 9110, quelles méthodes HTTP sont « sûres » (safe), c'est-à-dire sans effet attendu sur l'état du serveur ? (plusieurs réponses) ¦ GET": {"q":"According to RFC 9110, which HTTP methods are “safe”, i.e. with no expected effect on the server's state? (several answers)","e":"GET, HEAD, OPTIONS and TRACE are defined as safe (read-only). PUT is idempotent but not safe: it modifies the resource, even if repeating it gives the same result.","a":["GET","HEAD","PUT","OPTIONS"]},
  "Quel code HTTP un serveur renvoie-t-il quand le corps de la requête dépasse la taille qu'il accepte (par exemple un envoi de fichier trop lourd) ? ¦ 413": {"q":"Which HTTP code does a server return when the request body exceeds the size it accepts (for example an upload of a file that is too large)?","e":"413 signals a request body that is too large; RFC 9110 now calls it “Content Too Large” (formerly “Payload Too Large”). With Nginx, it's often the client_max_body_size directive that triggers this error.","placeholder":"3-digit code…","answers":["413","413 Content Too Large","413 Payload Too Large"]},
  "Quelle redirection temporaire garantit que le navigateur rejoue la même méthode et le même corps (un POST reste un POST) ? ¦ 307 Temporary Redirect": {"q":"Which temporary redirect guarantees that the browser replays the same method and the same body (a POST stays a POST)?","e":"307 forbids changing the method, whereas with 302 browsers have historically turned a POST into a GET. 303 forces a GET, and 308 is the permanent equivalent of 307.","a":["307 Temporary Redirect","302 Found","303 See Other","308 Permanent Redirect"]},
  "Remets dans l'ordre les étapes du flux OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code avec PKCE : ¦ Le client génère un code_verifier et redirige vers /authorize avec le code_challenge dérivé": {"q":"Put the steps of the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code flow with PKCE in order:","e":"PKCE binds the authorization code to the client that started the flow: only the party that knows the original code_verifier can exchange the code for tokens. An intercepted code therefore becomes unusable.","items":["The client generates a code_verifier and redirects to /authorize with the derived code_challenge","The user authenticates with the authorization server and gives consent","The server redirects to the redirect_uri with an authorization code","The client exchanges the code and the code_verifier for tokens at /token","The client calls the API with the access token in the Authorization: Bearer header"]},
  "Un cookie dont le nom commence par __Host- n'est accepté par le navigateur que s'il… ¦ Porte HttpOnly et SameSite=Strict": {"q":"A cookie whose name starts with __Host- is only accepted by the browser if it…","e":"The __Host- prefix requires Secure, an HTTPS origin, Path=/ and no Domain: the cookie is thus locked to the exact host and a subdomain can't overwrite it. __Secure- only requires Secure.","a":["Has HttpOnly and SameSite=Strict","Has the Secure attribute, Path=/ and no Domain attribute","Sets Domain to the parent domain to cover subdomains","Is set by a server on port 443 with Max-Age"]}
});
